# Copy this file to .env and fill in ONE of the two credentials below.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Which one depends on what you have. The guide has a section for each — open the
# one that applies to you and ignore the other entirely.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A CLAUDE SUBSCRIPTION (most people). On your OWN machine, not in the container:
#
#   npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code    (if you do not have it)
#   claude setup-token
#
# It opens a browser, you sign in, and it prints a long-lived token starting
# sk-ant-oat01-. Paste that value here.
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AN ANTHROPIC API KEY instead, from https://console.anthropic.com. Starts
# sk-ant-api03-. Uncomment the line and paste it here, and leave
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN above EMPTY.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TWO ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE, and they look almost identical: both start
# sk-ant- and both run to about 108 characters. Only the prefix tells them apart.
# Put one on the other's line and it fails silently — .env looks right, all six
# checks pass, and Claude asks you to log in anyway. The environment check tests
# the prefix for exactly that reason.

# The port the workshop app is served on, and the port the environment check
# proves is free. One value governs both, so if you change it you change it ONCE
# and every command in the guide stays exactly as written.
#
# Only change it if ./verify-setup.sh tells you 5173 is already in use. It is
# Vite's default port, so another project of yours may already have it.
# WORKSHOP_PORT=5173
